{"id":4411,"date":"2021-12-06T15:22:56","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:22:56","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4411"},"modified":"2024-11-11T13:46:23","modified_gmt":"2024-11-11T21:46:23","slug":"3-3-temperature-and-air-pressure-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-3-temperature-and-air-pressure-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"3.3: Temperature and Air Pressure"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b597c74\" name=\"6a2213b597c74\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b597db7\" name=\"6a2213b597db7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Describe what temperature is measured by the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s temperature sensor<\/li>\n<li>Explain the importance of the temperature measurement<\/li>\n<li>Create a graph that shows temperature increasing over time<\/li>\n<li>Convert temperature measurements to different units<\/li>\n<li>Describe what a barometer is and how it can be used to measure height<\/li>\n<li>Use barometer measurements to predict the height of the drone<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Temperature Sensor<\/li>\n<li>Print the Current Temperature<\/li>\n<li>Temperature in Flight<\/li>\n<li>Celsius to Fahrenheit<\/li>\n<li>Barometer<\/li>\n<li>Print the Current Pressure<\/li>\n<li>More Pressure Readings<\/li>\n<li>Pressure Readings and Lists<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Height Calculator Part 1<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Height Calculator Part 2<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b597e14\" name=\"6a2213b597e14\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Temperature Sensor<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One of the sensors onboard CoDrone EDU is the <strong>temperature sensor<\/strong>, which does exactly what it says: it reads the temperature. However, it does <strong>NOT<\/strong> get the temperature from its environment, so it cannot be used to tell you whether it\u2019s warm or cold where you are. What it can tell you is its internal temperature, so how cold or hot it is inside the drone. This number will be in degrees Celsius.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b597e7f\" name=\"6a2213b597e7f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Print the Current Temperature<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Find out what your CoDrone EDU\u2019s starting temperature is! Create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">temperature<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">that will store the drone\u2019s temperature after using the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_drone_temperature()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function. Next, include a print statement that will print <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">temperature<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u2019s value in degrees Celsius.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\ndrone = Drone()\ndrone.pair()\n\ntemperature = drone.get_drone_temperature()\n\nprint(temperature)\n\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Run the program to see how hot it is inside your drone. <em>Are you surprised by the results?<\/em><\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b597ee7\" name=\"6a2213b597ee7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Celsius to Fahrenheit<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fun fact: most of the world uses Celsius to measure temperature, and under 10% of countries use Fahrenheit. <\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/worldpopulationreview.com\/country-rankings\/countries-that-use-fahrenheit\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you\u2019re in one of the Fahrenheit countries<\/span><\/a><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, or if you just want your CoDrone EDU to do some extra math, you can write a function to convert the Celsius input to degrees Fahrenheit.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def to_fahrenheit(degrees_c):\n\n    degrees_f = degrees_c * 1.8 + 32\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 return degrees_f<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Try including this function in one of your programs! For example:<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\ndrone = Drone()\ndrone.pair()\n\ndef to_fahrenheit(degrees_c):\n    degrees_f = degrees_c * 1.8 + 32\n    return degrees_f\n\ntemperature = drone.get_drone_temperature()\n\nprint(\"Celsius: \", temperature)\nprint(\"Fahrenheit: \", to_fahrenheit(temperature))\n\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b597f90\" name=\"6a2213b597f90\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Barometer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In addition to the temperature sensor, the <strong>barometer<\/strong> can also measure pressure. You may have noticed that when you&#8217;re in the mountains or on a plane, your ears pop! The reason this happens is because there is air trapped in your inner ear. When you move up to a higher altitude, the air pressure is lower. This change causes pressure on your eardrum, which sounds and feels a lot like popping.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Our ears only pop with big differences in altitude. It probably wouldn&#8217;t happen if you went from the first floor of a building to the second floor. However, your CoDrone has a pressure sensor, also known as a barometer, built in to measure the air pressure around the CoDrone EDU. That sensor can detect seemingly tiny differences in height. Barometers are often used in weather forecasting, since changes in air pressure can affect the weather. For example, a drop in atmospheric pressure means a storm is most likely on the way, while an increase in pressure usually predicts clear skies.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b598000\" name=\"6a2213b598000\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Print the Current Pressure<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Find out what your CoDrone EDU\u2019s starting pressure is! Create a variable called <\/span><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">pressure<\/code><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> that will store the drone\u2019s air pressure after using the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_pressure()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function. Next, include a print statement that will print <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">pressure<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u2019s value in Pascals, which is the international standard for air pressure measurement.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\n\ndrone = Drone()\n\ndrone.pair()\n\npressure = drone.get_pressure()\n\nprint(\"Pressure: \", pressure)\n\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try running your program with the CoDrone EDU at different altitudes. <em>What do you notice about the difference in air pressure?<\/em><\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b598084\" name=\"6a2213b598084\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>More Pressure Readings<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Let\u2019s take what you learned and see it in action! Import the time library.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create a pressure variable that will get and store the air pressure at ground level. <em>Important: you will be creating multiple pressure variables, one for each height you want to measure the air pressure at. You should either make their names memorable (like a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">pressure_ground<\/code>\u00a0<\/em><\/span><em><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">) or use numbers (like \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">pressure_1<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">).<\/span><\/em><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Program your drone to take off and then create another pressure variable. This will get the air pressure at the takeoff altitude.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Have your drone throttle up at 50% speed for 1 second. Create another pressure variable that will get their air pressure at this level.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can keep repeating the last step until you have all the altitudes you need, or your drone hits the ceiling \u2014 whatever comes first!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Finally, have your drone land. You should then include print statements so you can see the air pressure at all the levels you included.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\n\nimport time\n\ndrone = Drone()\n\ndrone.pair()\n\n\npressure_1 = drone.get_pressure() # ground level\n\n\ndrone.takeoff()\n\npressure_2 = drone.get_pressure() # after takeoff level 1\n\n\ndrone.set_throttle(30)\n\ndrone.move(1)\n\npressure_3 = drone.get_pressure() # level 2\n\n\ndrone.move(1)\n\npressure_4 = drone.get_pressure() # level 3\n\n\ndrone.land()\n\n\nprint(\"On the Ground: \", pressure_1)\n\nprint(\"Level 1: \", pressure_2)\n\nprint(\"Level 2: \", pressure_3)\n\nprint(\"Level 3: \", pressure_4)\n\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b5980d7\" name=\"6a2213b5980d7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: Height Calculator Part 1<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, we will first take, store and print barometer measurements. We will then attempt to correlate those to the height of the drone.<\/p>\n<p>Define a function that takes readings and appends them to a list of pressures every second. You must use a loop to accomplish this. The function should take in a duration or number of readings as a parameter. Have the drone take off, call the function for a time of your choosing.<\/p>\n<p>Have the drone throttle up at a throttle level of your choosing for the duration that you chose (it is recommended to use a small number for both). Finally, use a loop to print the values stored in the pressures. When you run your program, use something to measure the height of the drone at takeoff and the approximate height at each 1 second interval (you could use the number of blocks on the wall from the floor or a measuring stick or even your body).[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b59812e\" name=\"6a2213b59812e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 2: Height Calculator Part 2<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that we have a list of pressures and heights, let&#8217;s figure out our conversion ratio. Complete the following calculation 3\u20134 sets of measurements you took:<\/p>\n<p><strong>height \/ pressure<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>This is your conversion ratio. Whenever you multiply this by a pressure, the Pascals will cancel and leave you with a height. Now that we know this ratio, we will program it into the second part of our program:<\/p>\n<p>Take your part 1 challenge program and add a function that takes in a list of pressure values and converts them to heights, and then prints the list. You will need two loops for this function.<\/p>\n<p>Add the function call at the end of the program and then change the value of the duration. Run the program and watch your drone. Now check the height values that were printed. <em>Do the values printed out correspond to how high your drone flew? Would changing the throttle change the ratio we created? Why or why not?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b598174\" name=\"6a2213b598174\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned how the CoDrone EDU can measure its internal temperature using its temperature sensor. You also learned about the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s barometer and how it can be used to measure the drone&#8217;s height.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 12 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-3-temperature-and-air-pressure-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4735,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1782,1848,1770,1793,1826,1438,1482,853,144,694,1392,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2158,2159,4328,14401,2156,2122,2134,2155,2157],"class_list":["post-4411","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-air-pressure","lesson-tag-barometer","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-intermediate","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-sensor","lesson-tag-temperature","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4411","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":35,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4411\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13219,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4411\/revisions\/13219"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4735"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4411"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4411"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4411"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}